ny political prisoners  [Applause]  the rallies were met with mass arrests  as protesters were dragged into police  vans one by  one politics in putin's russia  increasingly looks  like rule by force  well for more now i'm joined by felix  light from the moscow  times felix it's good to have you on the  program i understand that you were at a  pro-alexei navalny  protest um tell us what did you see  yes well so the the outlook among the  protesters tonight  among the navalny movement was in some  ways a little bit mournful  because after really dominating russian  politics russian opposition politics for  some years now  the navalny movement is facing some  really serious  legal penalties that will essentially  criminalize any involvement with navalny  today's crowd was was big but it wasn't  massive it was  certainly large for moscow standards we  saw certainly perhaps  north of uh of 10 000 people on the  streets of moscow at least today  and it was a crowd of young people of  old people of really sort of cutting  across the coalition of navalny's  support  but there was really was this  recognition that this is perhaps the end  of the line for sort of mass protest  activity in moscow for the time being  we hear that police detained hundreds of  protesters  can you confirm that did you see any of  that happening  i didn't personally see it myself and in  fact though there is there are reliable  reports of several hundred detentions  today  this was already almost rather uh  restrained by the standards of russian  policing certainly in january we saw  really aggressive sort of thorough going  policing and in some cases really savage  violence being meted out to protesters  today we saw less of that and that is  perhaps a deliberate decision by the  kremlin to sort of  take it a little bit easy now and what  about the the health of  alexi navalny the fact that we were  hearing that  he could die at any moment what impact  is that having on  the opposition movement in russia  well certainly it spurred a lot of  people to the streets today you know  many people were saying that they were  there specifically to protest what they  call the killing of navalny and  certainly  we do know that navalny's health is  extremely is well it's hanging by a  thread as one of his doctors has said  um he's of course on a hunger strike  he's also recuperating from uh  poisoning by novichok about about six  months ago so there is a great deal of  reason to be concerned about his health  strangely though in an instagram post  which was ferried out by his lawyers  yesterday  he almost told people not to worry  saying that you know this this potassium  buildup which his lawyers have  have flagged up as potentially causing  cardiac arrest is not to be worried  about and that he  doesn't believe that he's on the brink  of death so we will see  we don't quite know what the state of  his health is and the information we're  getting out of the prison is rather  fragmentary  okay correspondent felix light with the  latest tonight from moscow felix thank  you  now if alexi navalny were to die in  prison would there be consequences for  the kremlin  montford weber is leader of the  center-right european people's  parliament in the european party rather  in the european parliament  he says consequences without a doubt  what we can do is to show to vladimir  putin and to his whole system  how high the price could be if things  went in a  will will go in a wrong direction that  is what we have to show  and that's why for me it's clear if  navalny will  really hopefully not but if this really  will happen that he will die  then i think for example that no stream  two cannot be  uh finished can it be finalized as a  project we have to  tell putin the price is high please  respect international  obligations and and rules yeah clear  words there from mr putin  to talk about that i'm joined now by  manuel zarzin he is a member of  germany's opposition green party he's  the greens spokesman on eastern  europe policy and he's a member of the  german parliament's foreign policy  committee mr tarzin it's good to have  you on the program  to get us started i'd like to read a  quote from the chairman of the foreign  policy  committee norbert rutkin he was quoted  in the new york times as saying  however embarrassing it is for me the  greens have the clearest  stance of all the parties on china and  russia  and he also said the greens are a much  more realistic and preferable partner  for us  on foreign policy nice words for your  party so  if the government asks you what it  should do  next about president putin what will  your answer be  well i think that what mr putin is  calling red lines is actually a red  herring it is clearly that he's trying  to  mobilize his population because he's  under  real criticism and they've got elections  on the autumn  and it's a real tough situation for this  regime for this system of putin  because the people are running away of  the system so they're trying to mobilize  to hold the arm population mobilized by  creating a foreign enemy do you think  he's bluffing though i mean we hear  statements about all of these russian  soldiers amassing along  the border with ukraine is that a  serious threat  or is putin just bluffing  it is for sure a serious threat  he's showing us a variety of options  which they have in hand  from a fast escalation whether even  putting  the ideas of the grounding of it by  helping  so-called own passport holders  in ukraine also he was telling today  that  it would be an asymmetrical uh response  by russia which is nothing else than  an escalation which he's offering  on the other hand it can be just also  that they are trying to technically  swift the power balance in the black sea  region  anyway whatever they're doing is a clear  confrontation in the clear provocation  and i'm sure it will not just go far  they will go on business well i want to  imagine  let's say